\section{A Call Whose Destination Is Not Yet Known}

An assembler can encode a branch within a known layout, but it cannot predict where the dynamic loader will map a shared object's code. Even the static linker that builds the executable may not know which eligible definition of a symbol the runtime lookup will select. Levine distinguishes traditional static linking, historical static shared libraries that already committed to addresses at link time, and dynamic shared libraries that resolve remaining symbolic references at runtime \cite[chapter~9]{levine2000linkers}. Lazy procedure binding delays some of that last category of work further: a function is looked up only when its linkage entry is first used \cite[chapter~10]{levine2000linkers}.

On x86-64 GNU/Linux, a conventional ELF call to an external function can go to a stub in the caller object's \emph{procedure linkage table} (PLT). The static linker knows the distance from the call site to that stub. The stub jumps indirectly through a slot in the caller object's \emph{global offset table} (GOT), which will hold an absolute address. Static linking thus resolves the call to an address within the object and leaves a dynamic relocation for the slot. The corresponding \texttt{R\_X86\_64\_JUMP\_SLOT} record identifies both the slot to fill and the dynamic symbol to look up. Neither the call instruction nor the stub text needs rewriting at runtime.

Levine illustrates this scheme with 32-bit Intel ELF: his lazy stub communicates a relocation-table byte offset, and the reserved GOT words are four bytes wide. The x86-64 System V processor supplement, in its Procedure Linkage Table subsection, shows a relocation \emph{index} into the table designated by \texttt{DT\_JMPREL} and uses eight-byte GOT words. These details are not interchangeable. The trace below models the traditional x86-64 stub, not an instruction sequence required of every linker; alternate PLT layouts and compiler-generated GOT-indirect calls can implement different call paths.

