\section{Resolve One Slot Without Changing the Call Site}

Take an illustrative caller with a PLT entry at \texttt{0x401030} and a slot at \texttt{0x404000}. At \texttt{0x401125}, a five-byte call pushes \texttt{0x40112a}. Assume \texttt{twice} is an ordinary function with a definition at \texttt{0x7f2200011120} in this process, and that its slot relocation has index \(0\) in this object's procedure relocation table.

\begin{verbatim}
call twice@PLT                 # pushes the caller's return PC
twice@PLT:
    jump through slot[twice]
    push 0                     # unresolved slot points here
    jump PLT0
PLT0:
    push object_cookie         # index 0 is local to this object
    jump runtime_resolver
runtime_resolver:
    save call state; read the object's relocation 0
    p = lookup(relocation.symbol, object_lookup_scope)
    slot[twice] = p            # cache in data, not in code
    restore state and remove resolver metadata
    jump p                     # original return PC stays put
\end{verbatim}

Initially the slot points to \texttt{0x401036}, the instruction after the first indirect jump in this stub layout. The first call, with argument \(21\), follows the slow path, updates the slot to \texttt{0x7f2200011120}, and returns \(42\) to \texttt{0x40112a}. A second call with argument \(1\) jumps through the updated slot and returns \(2\), without going through the resolver. The resolver must preserve ABI-defined argument state (including any applicable vector arguments) and deliver the original return address and expected stack shape to the callee. This is a single-thread trace; the loader must also make binding safe if multiple threads reach the slot concurrently.

\Cref{fig:visual-297} contrasts the two routes through the same PLT entry.

