\section{Move the Lookup, Not the Dependency}

Lazy binding can avoid lookups for procedures never called in a particular run. It does not generally defer mapping ordinary \texttt{DT\_NEEDED} dependencies, or defer data relocations that have no call stub to mediate their first use. Eager binding fills the procedure slots before ordinary execution of the object rather than waiting for calls. It trades startup work for predictable first-call latency and reports a missing required symbol when the object is loaded rather than when an optional code path finally exercises it. On GNU toolchains, \texttt{-z now} requests eager binding for the linked object, and on glibc a nonempty \texttt{LD\_BIND\_NOW} can request it at process startup. With a suitable \texttt{-z relro} layout, resolving all slots early also lets the loader make their pages read-only after relocation.

\section{Measure When Binding Happens}

In an otherwise clean test directory on x86-64 GNU/Linux with glibc, put \verb|int twice(int x) { return 2*x; }| in \texttt{twice.c} and the following in \texttt{main.c}:

\begin{verbatim}
extern int twice(int);
int main(void) { return twice(21) + twice(1) != 44; }
\end{verbatim}

\begin{verbatim}
gcc -fPIC -shared -o libtwice.so twice.c
gcc -fplt main.c -L. -ltwice -Wl,-rpath,'$ORIGIN',-z,lazy -o lazy
gcc -fplt main.c -L. -ltwice -Wl,-rpath,'$ORIGIN',-z,now -o now
readelf -Wr lazy now
readelf -Wd lazy now
env -u LD_BIND_NOW LD_DEBUG=bindings ./lazy
LD_BIND_NOW=1 LD_DEBUG=bindings ./lazy
\end{verbatim}

The two binaries both contain a \texttt{JUMP\_SLOT} for \texttt{twice}; its presence alone does not establish when it is filled. In the local test the eager binary records a \texttt{NOW} flag, while the lazy binary does not. Look for the glibc binding line for \texttt{twice} relative to the line reporting transfer to the program: it follows the transfer in the first run and precedes it in the second. Exit status \(0\) in both runs checks the values, not their binding times. If the required library is absent and not found in any search directory, neither build can start; lazy binding does not turn a required library into an optional plugin.
